3. Archetypes — Attractors in Narrative State Space

An archetype is not simply “a kind of character.”

It is a relatively stable behavioral/relational attractor.

Let an actor possess state:

a_t =
(
goal,
belief,
identity,
resources,
policy,
relationships
)

An archetype H defines an attractor basin:

\boxed{
a_{t+1}=H(a_t,N_t)
}

Across different stories, surface details may vary enormously while behavior repeatedly converges toward similar patterns.

Thus:

\boxed{
Archetype =
stable policy geometry under recurring conditions
}

⸻

4. Example Archetypes

The Hero

Invariant:

Accepts increasing cost in pursuit of an objective whose significance expands beyond immediate self-interest.

Mathematically:

\max_\pi
\mathbb{E}
[
GoalAchievement
+
Meaning
-
Cost
]

subject to:

Cost_t \uparrow

and continued commitment.

Pseudo-interface:

HERO(
    actor,
    objective,
    stakes,
    cost
)
→ committed_actor

Failure mode:

Hero becomes indistinguishable from reckless persistence when the objective can no longer survive Reality Stitch.

⸻

The Mentor

Invariant:

Transfers capability while reducing permanent dependence on the mentor.

Capability(Learner)_{t+1}
>
Capability(Learner)_t

while ideally:

Dependency(Learner,Mentor)_{t+1}
<
Dependency_t

Pseudo-interface:

MENTOR(
    learner,
    missing_capability,
    demonstration,
    constraint
)
→ increased_independence

The best mentor eventually makes itself less necessary.

⸻

The Trickster

Invariant:

Introduces perturbation that exposes hidden assumptions or rigid structure.

S'
=
S+\epsilon

where \epsilon is strategically disruptive variation.

TRICKSTER(
    system,
    taboo,
    hidden_assumption
)
→ destabilized_representation

Fitness depends upon what the disturbance reveals.

Trickster without metabolism becomes noise.

⸻

The Guardian

Invariant:

Controls passage across a consequential boundary.

Access(X)
=
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if threshold satisfied}\\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

GUARDIAN(
    candidate,
    threshold,
    protected_boundary
)
→ pass | reject | test

Guardian is essentially a narrative gate.

⸻

The Shadow

Invariant:

Externalizes a suppressed, rejected, feared, or adversarial possibility.

Let:

Self=S

and rejected latent structure:

L\subset Potential(S)

Then:

SHADOW(S)
\rightarrow
Externalize(L)

The Shadow operator allows a system to reason against possibilities it does not comfortably hold inside its active identity.

SHADOW(
    self_model,
    suppressed_traits
)
→ adversarial_projection

Useful for Red Teaming.

Dangerous when projection is mistaken for external observation.

⸻

The Fool

Invariant:

Gains access to possibility space because it is less constrained by the prevailing model.

ConstraintSet_{fool}
\subset
ConstraintSet_{orthodoxy}

so:

ReachableStates_{fool}
\supset
ReachableStates_{orthodoxy}

The Fool is therefore a search operator.

Not intelligence through ignorance.

Freedom through reduced representational commitment.

⸻

The Ruler

Invariant:

Optimizes continuity of a system through authority allocation.

RULER:
State
\rightarrow
GovernedState

subject to:

Continuity
+
Order
+
Legitimacy

Failure mode:

Preservation(System)
\rightarrow
Preservation(Position)

The archetype flips when the steward begins optimizing for the control structure rather than what the control structure exists to preserve.

