21. Plot Devices — Transition Machinery

A plot device exists principally to force or enable a state change.

Examples:

MacGuffin

Creates shared objective attraction.

MACGUFFIN(X)
\rightarrow
AlignGoals(A_1,...,A_n)

The object itself may possess little intrinsic semantic importance.

Its function is goal synchronization.

⸻

Deus Ex Machina

Injects an external transition not sufficiently generated by prior causal state.

N_t
\xrightarrow{ExternalCause}
N_{t+1}

with low causal preparation:

P(N_{t+1}\mid N_{0:t})
\ll 1.

Useful as a diagnostic:

Did the solution emerge from the architecture or get imported from outside?

⸻

Chekhov’s Gun

Registers a latent dependency.

Plant(X,t_0)

then later:

Activate(X,t_n).

Constraint:

Identity(X_{t_0})\approx Identity(X_{t_n}).

This is delayed causal persistence.

⸻

Red Herring

Allocates attention to a plausible but misleading hypothesis.

Attention(H_f)\uparrow

while:

P(H_f=true)\downarrow.

Useful operator for studying search robustness.

⸻

Foreshadowing

Injects partial predictive information before causal realization.

Info(E_{future})>0

while:

Certainty(E_{future})<1.

Foreshadowing trains pattern anticipation without full disclosure.

⸻

22. Conflict — Competing Gradients

Conflict occurs when simultaneous objectives cannot all be satisfied under current constraints.

Let:

G_1,G_2,...,G_n

be objectives.

Conflict exists when:

\not\exists S':
G_1(S')\land G_2(S')\land...\land G_n(S')

under current resources and rules.

Thus narrative tension can be modeled as:

\boxed{
Tension
\propto
ConstraintPressure
\times
GoalImportance
\times
Uncertainty.
}

Story movement occurs because the current state is unstable relative to competing gradients.

⸻

23. Internal Conflict

One actor may hold incompatible objectives:

G_{self}
\neq
G_{role}.

or:

Value_A
\neq
Value_B.

Then:

Conflict_{internal}
=
Gradient(G_A,G_B).

Character decisions reveal which objective receives higher effective weight.

This is a useful model for decision architecture.

