33. Reversal

A reversal occurs when new information or action flips expected gradient.

Expected:

S_t\rightarrow S_a.

Observed:

S_t\rightarrow S_b.

where:

Distance(S_a,S_b)

is large.

A good reversal is surprising locally but reconstructable retrospectively.

Thus:

\boxed{
GoodTwist
=
HighSurprise
+
HighRetrospectiveCoherence
}

Too predictable:

Surprise\approx0.

Too arbitrary:

Coherence\approx0.

⸻

34. Reveal

A reveal changes knowledge state without necessarily changing external reality.

World_t=World_{t+1}

but:

K_t\neq K_{t+1}.

Therefore:

\boxed{
Narrative state can change dramatically while physical state remains constant.
}

This matters directly to Pantheon.

Representation change can alter available action without external state changing.

⸻

35. Dramatic Irony

Knowledge topology:

K_{audience}
\supset
K_{actor}.

This generates predicted consequence the actor cannot yet perceive.

Dramatic irony is therefore an exercise in asymmetric models.

It can train reasoning about:

* hidden information,
* adversarial awareness,
* future consequence,
* observer-relative knowledge.

