36. Suspense

Suspense is approximately:

Suspense
=
Stakes
\times
Uncertainty
\times
AnticipatedProximity.

The audience knows enough to predict meaningful possibility but not enough to resolve it.

Too little information:

confusion.

Too much:

certainty.

Suspense occupies the useful middle.

Interestingly similar to Semantic Momentum.

Enough structure to continue predicting.

Not enough to collapse the future.

⸻

37. Mystery

Mystery reverses the normal causal direction.

Usually:

Cause\rightarrow Effect.

Mystery begins with:

Effect
\rightarrow ?

and performs inverse inference:

\boxed{
Cause^*
=
\arg\max_C P(C\mid Evidence)
}

Mystery is therefore narrative abduction.

⸻

38. Prophecy

Prophecy introduces information from a possible future into current decision state.

K_t
\leftarrow
Information(N_{t+n}).

But knowledge of the prediction changes behavior:

Prediction
\rightarrow
Action
\rightarrow
Future.

So prophecy stories naturally explore reflexive prediction systems.

A prediction becomes causal because it is known.

This maps directly to metrics, forecasts, labels, and Definition Stewardship.

⸻

39. The Self-Fulfilling Prophecy Operator

Prediction(P)
\rightarrow
Behavior(P)
\rightarrow
IncreaseLikelihood(P).

Pseudo-code:

SELF_FULFILLING_PROPHECY(
    belief,
    actor
):
    policy = adapt_to(belief)
    world_delta = execute(policy)
    return update_probability(belief, world_delta)

Useful far outside narrative.

